    "body": "ROME — Italy has strongly denied a press report suggesting it opposes granting Germany partner status on the GCAP fighter program, claiming instead it fully backs German membership.\nThe unusually long rebuttal from the Italian ministry of defense followed a report in the Financial Times on Saturday that defense minister Guido Crosetto used a video conference call last week with other representatives of the Italian-UK-Japan fighter to oppose making Germany a member.\nBerlin is mulling signing up to the program after the collapse of its talks with France to develop a rival sixth-generation jet.\nThe press report suggested Italy was pushing to hand Germany observer status on the program rather than full membership, although it did quote an Italian official saying German membership could follow.\nWithin hours of the report appearing, Crosetto’s ministry fired off a statement calling the article “entirely misleading”, adding it “does not reflect the position Italy has consistently maintained.”\nThe statement agreed that it wanted Germany to obtain observer status, but in order to accelerate full membership status, not to delay or block it.\n“Italy has always expressed its full willingness to welcome Germany into the program. Indeed, precisely to facilitate and accelerate this process, Italy proposed that Germany joins as an observer, as an immediate, Program information-sharing enabler towards subsequent full membership,” it added.\nThe statement added, “This proposal was not intended to limit Germany’s future role in the program. Quite the opposite: it was designed to speed up the conditions for its involvement as quickly as possible, while safeguarding the program’s progress and established timetable.”\nThe talks come as the GCAP program picks up speed after the three nation partners handed a £4.6 billion ($6.1 billion) development contract to their national champions building the jet in July.\nRome has spent €875 million ($970 million) this year on development .\nCanada has meanwhile already taken observer status on the program.\nThe Italian statement said, “It is therefore totally false any reporting on opposition to Germany’s participation. Italy has demonstrated, through its actions and proposals, that it considers Germany’s involvement a positive opportunity.”\nIt added, “Our priority remains ensuring that GCAP proceeds according to its agreed schedule, while creating the conditions for new partners to join through a clear and effective process. Italy is supporting, without delay, all the conditions to welcome Germany’s participation in the GCAP as soon as possible.”",
